  "articleBody": "Getting a drywall patch wrong can leave obvious lumps, loose tape, and cracking seams that show through new paint. Here are common mistakes property owners encounter and how to avoid them:\n\n- Using mismatched sheetrock thickness: Avoid uneven joints by confirming your contractor matches your wall's exact drywall depth.\n- Fastening screws too close to the drywall edge: This crumbles the gypsum core. Fasteners must stay back from edges into sturdy wood backing.\n- Laying down one thick coat of mud: Heavy mud shrinks and cracks. The right approach is applying multiple thin coats, allowing full drying between each layer.\n- Neglecting to center tape over seam lines: Uncentered seam tape shifts under pressure and leads to hairline fissures down the line.\n- Skipping dedicated primer before painting: Raw compound absorbs topcoat paint differently than surrounding paper, causing flashing marks. Always seal patches with primer first.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nIf your damaged drywall sits near active plumbing, electrical boxes, or textured ceilings, fixing it right requires hands-on experience. At HH Remodeling LLC, Abraham Becerra provides dependable drywall repairs and finish carpentry for Denver area homes. Call 303-505-0866 or email Hhremodelingllc1986.ab@gmail.com to discuss your project.",
